Z||000006||Cheever3000||11-02-2001||12:37 PM||||Back to the subject.<BR>I love all Fieros regardless of condition.  I was fortunate to acquire two 88's in primo shape, but it looks to me like most of the  Fieros I see are barely hanging together.  I would <I>rather</I> they all be nice & pretty, but it's not always the current owner's fault.  I tip my hat figuratively to all of them, because I know they love driving theirs - even tho it looks like a pile - as much as I love driving mine.  And there, but for the grace of God, go I.  I'm barely able to afford to keep mine running myself.  Got one sick now in fact, and the other's been complaining.  And with a few mishaps here & there, neither of mine are as pretty as when I purchased them.  That breaks my heart, but the point is I'm doing all I can for them.  Sounds like they're my children, doesn't it?  Well...<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Voytek||11-02-2001||12:50 PM||||First off, 84Fierotrevor - I know the feeling.  It doesn't take long; soon you'll be able to recognize Fieros at night, in the rearview mirror just by their headlights.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>As far as grammar, sorry but I'll have to agree with Stimpy on this one.  Run-on sentences and a general lack of punctuation make for unattractive looking posts, not to mention the fact that they take longer to read.  They are also a reflection of the postee, whether true or false.<P>Spelling mistakes?  Yeah, we all make them when we type fast.  Proofreading takes on less importance when on PFF but we all have had some degree of schooling and should at least attempt to make our posts legible.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
